# PART 2 — COMPLETE CATEGORY SYSTEM AUDIT AND REPAIR

## 3. The current problem

There is currently a mismatch between the categories displayed in the Admin Panel and the categories available in the external customer-facing Catalog.

Example:

- The Admin Panel contains a category named "ყურსასმენები".
- The customer-facing Catalog does not display the same category.

This indicates that category management and catalog display may be using different data sources, different category definitions, inconsistent IDs, or different filtering logic.

Do not simply add "ყურსასმენები" manually to the customer-facing screen.

Investigate and repair the underlying architecture so this mismatch cannot happen again.

## 4. Full category-system inspection

Inspect the actual implementation of:

- Admin category management screen.
- Customer-facing category screen.
- Product creation form.
- Product editing form.
- Product model.
- Category model.
- Firestore category collection.
- Firestore product collection.
- Category repository.
- Product repository.
- Category IDs and references.
- Category filtering and visibility logic.
- Product type fields.
- Existing category sorting and display order.
- Active/inactive category handling.
- Category image/icon handling.
- Existing product-to-category relationships.

Identify whether the current application uses:
- Hardcoded category arrays.
- Separate category lists for Admin Panel and Catalog.
- Different category names for the same category.
- Different IDs for equivalent categories.
- Product category names instead of stable category IDs.
- Incorrect filtering of categories with zero products.
- Inconsistent active/visible status handling.
- Missing or outdated category references.

Inspect actual Firestore documents and code paths where possible.

Do not guess the current category structure.

## 5. Establish one canonical category source

This is the most important architectural requirement.

The entire application must use ONE canonical category data source.

The Admin Panel and customer-facing Catalog must never maintain independent category definitions.

Use the existing Firestore architecture and existing collections where possible.

Do not create duplicate category systems.

Each category must have a stable unique ID.

Recommended logical fields, adapted to the existing model:

- id
- name
- slug
- description (optional)
- icon or image reference (optional)
- productType or categoryType (if useful)
- isActive
- isVisible
- sortOrder
- createdAt
- updatedAt

Do not add unnecessary fields if equivalent existing fields already exist.

Use stable category IDs instead of relying only on category display names.

The display name may change without breaking product references.

The Admin Panel must manage the canonical category records.

The customer-facing Catalog must load those same records from the same repository/source.

There must not be a separate hardcoded customer-facing category list.

## 6. Synchronize Admin Panel and Catalog

Implement consistent category visibility and status rules.

Requirements:

- Categories created in the Admin Panel must become available to the customer-facing Catalog according to their active/visible status.
- Category edits must appear in the Catalog without requiring an application update.
- Category activation/deactivation must be respected.
- Sorting must be consistent.
- Category IDs must match between products and category records.
- The Catalog must not display duplicate categories.
- The Admin Panel must show the real canonical category list.

Clearly distinguish:

1. All categories managed by the administrator.
2. Active categories.
3. Categories visible to customers.
4. Categories with products.
5. Categories currently empty.

A category can exist in the Admin Panel without appearing in the customer Catalog if it is intentionally inactive or hidden.

However, this must be an explicit status, not an accidental mismatch.

Show useful category information in the Admin Panel, such as product count, active/hidden status, and display order, when supported by real data.

If a category is active and customer-visible, its customer-facing behavior must be predictable and consistent.

Do not silently hide a category because of an unrelated filtering bug.

If an active visible category has zero products, handle it using a clearly defined rule and show an appropriate empty state if it is displayed.

